ueries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n### Memory Flush (15:25)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (18:30)\n\n**Context:** Eric moved all three data pulls (GPS, Perch/PlayerProfiles, VALD/ForcePlate) from the Windows laptop to the Mac, then ran closeout and verified the first afternoon scheduled runs.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Windows verification step: after `git pull` in `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\_INBOX`, `Select-String -Path refresh_all_data.bat -Pattern \"skipped\"` must return 3 lines (ForcePlate, GPS, Perch). Eric confirmed done.\n- \"Can I turn VALD off on Windows?\" — already off; the disabled step 1 in `refresh_all_data.bat` IS the VALD step, and Windows VALD had been broken since 8/2 anyway.\n- Afternoon receipt check: all 3 pulls finished exit 0, but Perch and VALD ran ~1 hour late.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **Skip rotating the exposed VALD client secret** — repo is private and pulls work. Noted as \"skip for now\" in `_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Mac owns all 3 pulls; Windows scheduled task still runs at 8 PM but pulls nothing.\n- Schedule: GPS 10:00/16:30, Perch 10:05/16:35, VALD 10:10/16:40.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Mac sleep is the real failure mode for scheduled pulls.** The Mac slept at 3:45 PM; GPS ran on time at 4:32, but Perch and VALD only crept forward during short background wakes and finished ~5:36–5:39 PM. Perch timed out twice before try 3 succeeded. Same root cause as the 6:30 AM GPS problem.\n- Data still arrived — retries saved it — but lateness is not reliable.\n- No failure alerts fired, so Perch/VALD alert paths remain unproven.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open decision:** set a daily wake with `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00` (recommended) vs. leave it. Not set yet. Undo is `sudo pmset repeat cancel`.\n- Perch and VALD failure alerts still untested.\n- Eric can delete the unused GPS database copy: `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n- Handoff written to `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-09-21-mac-owns-data-pulls.md`; STATUS.md updated in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`, `PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026`, `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem`, `_INBOX`.\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"ecff9cb1-3f4e-4f95-becb-f41b76aa3d84","version":"2.1.278","gitBranch":"main"}
